<!--
  DEMO-ONLY vehicle types (STEP 1 + STEP 1 REFINEMENT, mixed-traffic realism).

  ============================================================================
  THIS FILE IS NEVER USED BY TRAINING OR EVALUATION. IT IS DEMO SCAFFOLDING.
  ============================================================================
  `sim/networks/vehicle_types.add.xml` remains the ONE file every training run,
  every evaluation sweep and the 4a bake-off load, and it is unchanged. Every
  recorded number in this project (the §16 baselines, the 4a bake-off grid,
  Tier 0's 41.0s worst wait, Stage 4's 0.00 starvation events on (4,3,2)) was
  measured against THAT file, on SUMO's default lane-disciplined LC2013 model.

  Vehicle dynamics here are different, so throughput, waits and starvation are
  different too. NEVER re-measure a checkpoint against this file and compare
  the result to a recorded number - they describe different worlds.
  `training/train.py` asserts the default file before `model.learn()`, so a
  training run cannot pick this up even by accident.

  Evidence base, tier rationale and the sourced-vs-reasoned split:
  docs/MIXED_TRAFFIC_RESEARCH.md. Read it before retuning anything here.

  ===========================================================================
  WHAT THIS MODELS, HONESTLY (§17 boundary language)
  ===========================================================================
  This approximates authentic mixed-traffic behaviour using SUMO's sublane
  model (SL2015), which is the best lever SUMO has. It is NOT true lane-free
  driving: the road is still a set of lanes with centre-lines and lane-to-lane
  connections. What SL2015 adds is a continuous LATERAL position, so vehicles
  can sit off-centre, straddle a lane boundary, and pass one another inside a
  single lane. Requires the lateral-resolution option on the command line.

  ===========================================================================
  DRIVER HETEROGENEITY (REFINEMENT) - three tiers per everyday type
  ===========================================================================
  `bike`, `auto` and `car` are <vTypeDistribution>s, not single vTypes, so not
  every bike behaves like every other bike. Splitting a class into weighted
  aggressiveness sub-types is standard SUMO practice (research §1.6).

  VERIFIED on SUMO 1.27.1 that the route file's own
  `<vTypeDistribution id="mixed" vTypes="bike auto car truck">` resolves
  correctly when its members are themselves distributions, so
  `sim/scenario_generator.py` needs NO change and the training route writer is
  untouched.

  THE SPLITS ARE A REASONED DEFAULT, NOT A SOURCED PROPORTION (research §2.1):

      bike   20% cautious / 45% normal / 35% aggressive
      auto   25% cautious / 50% normal / 25% aggressive
      car             (none) / 90% normal / 10% aggressive
      truck / ambulance   single type, unchanged

  The AGGRESSIVE tier is STEP 1's own measured, signed-off table, kept as a
  CEILING - the refinement only adds calmer traffic below it, so it cannot
  make the most aggressive behaviour worse than what was already verified.
  Two deliberate exceptions, both recorded:
    * bike tau 0.9 -> 1.0 (see the tau block below)
    * lcTimeToImpatience 5.0 -> 20.0 on bike, 8.0 -> 20.0 on auto (see the
      overtaking block below)

  ===========================================================================
  THE OVERTAKING FIX - why lcSpeedGain was NOT the lever
  ===========================================================================
  Measured (research §3.1): STEP 1's lane-change WISH was already correctly
  gated by speed differential - the LCA_SPEEDGAIN rate ran 0.0126 -> 0.2973
  across the delta range, a 23.6x span, near zero where there was no speed to
  gain. `lcSpeedGain` was doing its job.

  What was NOT gated was EXECUTION. Completed passes sat flat at 17-31 per
  1000 moving follower-steps across every delta bucket, with the "leader is
  already at or above my desired speed" bucket as high as the "leader is 4-6
  m/s slower" one. The cause is visible in the blocked rate: 0.007-0.19 in the
  demo against 0.63-0.71 in the baseline. Essentially every wish the model
  formed, however worthless, was executed.

  So the levers here are the ones that decide whether a wish BECOMES a
  manoeuvre - lcAssertive (gap acceptance), lcPushy (lateral encroachment),
  lcSublane (reluctance to reposition) - and above all the impatience pair.
  lcImpatience dynamically AMPLIFIES lcAssertive and lcPushy, and
  lcTimeToImpatience=5.0 drove the model to maximum impatience after five
  seconds, erasing the speed-gain threshold it had just computed correctly.
  Raised to 20.0 so a real delay is required before the threshold erodes.

  Cautious tiers sit at or near SUMO stock, so a low-value wish is refused
  outright rather than forced through. That is what restores the delta
  response at the OUTCOME level, not just at the wish level.

  ===========================================================================
  tau MUST be >= STEP_LENGTH_S (1.0) - a NUMERICAL bound, not a style choice
  ===========================================================================
  Krauss's safe-velocity cannot guarantee a collision-free follow below the
  simulation step size. STEP 1 measured 272 of 1870 vehicles (14.55%) in a
  collision at bike tau=0.5 / auto tau=0.6, against 0.00% after raising them.

  STEP 1 nonetheless shipped bike tau=0.9, below the bound its own rule
  states, and SUMO warned about it on every load. The refinement raises the
  aggressive bike tier to 1.0, closing that violation. NO TIER MAY GO BELOW
  1.0 for a behavioural reason - research §1.4 says two-wheelers accept the
  smallest gaps, and this bound is where that expression stops. Realism loses
  to numerical validity here. If you ever lower tau again, RE-MEASURE
  collisions; do not assume.

  ===========================================================================
  RED-LIGHT BEHAVIOUR (jm*) IS GATED TO THE AGGRESSIVE TIER ONLY
  ===========================================================================
  Research §1.5: a crash-causation study attributed 72.5% of contributing
  factors to over-speeding against 0.7% for red-light jumping. That is a
  crash-factor breakdown, NOT a jump-rate, and is used only directionally:
  signal violation is an occasional behaviour of a minority, not universal.

  Every cautious and normal tier therefore sets jmDriveAfterRedTime="-1"
  (never enters on red) and jmIgnoreFoeProb="0.0". Only the aggressive tiers
  carry a permissive jm* group, and car's aggressive minority gets a much
  smaller allowance than bike's or auto's. Truck and ambulance never run a
  red at all.

  Resulting red-eligible population, against the route file's own mix
  (bike .15 / auto .25 / car .50 / truck .10):

      bike   0.15 x 0.35 = 5.25%
      auto   0.25 x 0.25 = 6.25%
      car    0.50 x 0.10 = 5.00%
      truck  0.10 x 0.00 = 0.00%
      ..............................
      total              = 16.50%   of everyday vehicles

  and ELIGIBLE is not the same as OFFENDING: jmDriveAfterRedTime is the
  window after the light turns red in which the vehicle will still enter, and
  jmIgnoreFoeProb is a per-encounter probability, so actual red entries are a
  fraction of that 16.50%.

  ===========================================================================
  lcMaxSpeedLatStanding IS LOAD-BEARING - do not drop it
  ===========================================================================
  It defaults to 0 for every vClass except bicycle/pedestrian, meaning a
  STOPPED vehicle cannot move sideways at all. Without it set explicitly,
  queue-front filtering is impossible for `auto` no matter what minGapLat and
  lcPushy say, because the filtering happens while the queue is stationary.
  Scaled by tier: a cautious driver still filters, just far less.

  ===========================================================================
  TWO SILENT BREAKAGES THE TIERING CREATES - both fixed in perception/
  ===========================================================================
  Recorded here because the fixes live in another module and would otherwise
  look unmotivated:

  1. traci.vehicle.getTypeID() returns the CONCRETE SUB-TYPE ("bike.normal"),
     not "bike". perception/lane_sensor.py matched that against VEHICLE_TYPES
     and would have counted every tiered vehicle as UNKNOWN, zeroing
     type_composition -> §9.2 observation features LF_TYPE_START+0..4.
     Fixed by resolving the base name before the match.

  2. traci.vehicletype.getTau("bike") / setTau("bike", ...) do NOT raise on a
     distribution id - they resolve to ONE RANDOMLY SAMPLED member. Measured:
     a read returned bike.aggressive's value while the following write landed
     on bike.normal, leaving 2 of 3 tiers on clear-weather dynamics.
     perception/weather.py drove §7.4 entirely through those calls, so weather
     would have become ~1/3 true, silently and non-reproducibly. Fixed by
     resolving each base type to its concrete members.

  Both fixes are inert on the default file, which has no dotted ids.

  ===========================================================================
  maxSpeed: recalibrated for a busy urban arterial, NOT a highway
  ===========================================================================
  Measured on the default file, 1200s, corridor 4/3/2: car reached 16.70 m/s
  (60.1 km/h) - its own maxSpeed, above the road's 50 km/h limit, because
  SUMO's default speedFactor spread allows ~1.2x. That is a highway desired
  speed on a 300m-spaced signalised arterial. Values below are desired
  free-flow speeds between junctions, which vehicles rarely reach.

  The ROAD limit (DEFAULT_SPEED_MPS = 13.89 m/s in generate_corridor.py) is
  NOT touched - changing it would require regenerating all 27 networks,
  including the ones every checkpoint trained on.

  `bike` is modelled behaviourally as a two-wheeler (speed, width, gap
  acceptance). vClass stays "bicycle" deliberately: changing it could change
  lane permissions and diverge this network's connectivity from the trained
  one, for zero behavioural gain. guiShape is untouched - sumo-gui's own
  rendering is explicitly out of scope.
-->
<additional>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         BIKE - 20 / 45 / 35. The most permissive class at every tier, but
         a cautious bike is a genuinely different road user from an
         aggressive one: it queues rather than filters, holds a real
         following gap, and never enters on red.
         ================================================================== -->
    <vTypeDistribution id="bike">

        <vType id="bike.cautious" probability="0.20"
               vClass="bicycle" guiShape="bicycle"
               length="1.8" width="0.65" minGap="1.2"
               maxSpeed="10.0" accel="1.8" decel="3.5"
               speedFactor="normc(0.92,0.08,0.80,1.05)"
               sigma="0.45" tau="1.3" startupDelay="0.5"
               laneChangeModel="SL2015"
               minGapLat="0.45" lcPushy="0.15" lcAssertive="1.1"
               lcSpeedGain="1.3" lcImpatience="0.0"
               lcKeepRight="0.0" lcSublane="1.0"
               maxSpeedLat="0.8" lcAccelLat="1.0"
               lcMaxSpeedLatStanding="0.25" lcMaxDistLatStanding="1.0"
               latAlignment="center"
               impatience="0.2"
               jmDriveAfterRedTime="-1" jmDriveAfterYellowTime="0.0"
               jmIgnoreFoeProb="0.0" jmTimegapMinor="1.5" jmStoplineGap="0.6" />

        <vType id="bike.normal" probability="0.45"
               vClass="bicycle" guiShape="bicycle"
               length="1.8" width="0.65" minGap="1.0"
               maxSpeed="10.5" accel="2.1" decel="3.5"
               speedFactor="normc(0.97,0.09,0.82,1.12)"
               sigma="0.55" tau="1.1" startupDelay="0.2"
               laneChangeModel="SL2015"
               minGapLat="0.28" lcPushy="0.50" lcAssertive="1.8"
               lcSpeedGain="2.3" lcImpatience="0.35" lcTimeToImpatience="30.0"
               lcKeepRight="0.0" lcSublane="0.6"
               maxSpeedLat="1.2" lcAccelLat="1.5"
               lcMaxSpeedLatStanding="0.5" lcMaxDistLatStanding="1.6"
               latAlignment="arbitrary"
               impatience="0.6"
               jmDriveAfterRedTime="-1" jmDriveAfterYellowTime="0.0"
               jmIgnoreFoeProb="0.0" jmTimegapMinor="1.0" jmStoplineGap="0.4" />

        <!-- STEP 1's signed-off bike table, kept as the ceiling. Two changes:
             tau 0.9 -> 1.0 (the STEP_LENGTH_S bound) and lcTimeToImpatience
             5.0 -> 20.0 (the execution-gating fix). -->
        <vType id="bike.aggressive" probability="0.35"
               vClass="bicycle" guiShape="bicycle"
               length="1.8" width="0.65" minGap="0.8"
               maxSpeed="11.0" accel="2.4" decel="3.5"
               speedFactor="normc(1.00,0.10,0.85,1.20)"
               sigma="0.65" tau="1.0" startupDelay="0.0"
               laneChangeModel="SL2015"
               minGapLat="0.15" lcPushy="0.90" lcAssertive="2.8"
               lcSpeedGain="4.0" lcImpatience="1.0" lcTimeToImpatience="20.0"
               lcKeepRight="0.0" lcSublane="0.2"
               maxSpeedLat="1.6" lcAccelLat="2.0"
               lcMaxSpeedLatStanding="0.8" lcMaxDistLatStanding="2.2"
               latAlignment="arbitrary"
               impatience="1.0"
               jmDriveAfterRedTime="1.0" jmDriveAfterYellowTime="4.0"
               jmDriveRedSpeed="7.0" jmIgnoreFoeProb="0.10" jmIgnoreFoeSpeed="3.0"
               jmTimegapMinor="0.8" jmStoplineGap="0.2" />

    </vTypeDistribution>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         AUTO - 25 / 50 / 25. Close behind the bike at matching tiers,
         demanding slightly more room throughout.
         ================================================================== -->
    <vTypeDistribution id="auto">

        <vType id="auto.cautious" probability="0.25"
               vClass="passenger" guiShape="passenger"
               length="3.2" width="1.4" minGap="1.8"
